# Polymorphism

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- **Polymorphism**: an object's ability to take different forms
- Essential ingredients of polymorphic behavior:
  - Ability to define a method in a superclass and override it in a subclass
    - Subclass defines method with the same name
  - Ability to call the correct version of the overridden method depending on
    the type of object that called it
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
- In previous inheritance examples showed how to override the `__init__()` method
  - Called superclass `__init__()` method and then added onto that
- The same can be done for any other method
  - The method can call the superclass equivalent and add to it, or do 
    something completely different
::::
:::

# The `isinstance()` Function

- Polymorphism provides great flexibility when designing programs
- **`AttributeError` exception**: raised when a method receives an object
  which is not an instance of the right class
- **`isinstance()` function**: determines whether object is an instance of a class
  - Format: `isinstance(object, class)`

# Summary

- Inheritance allows a new class to extend an existing class.  The new class
  inherits the members (data and methods) of the class it extends.
- The "is a" relationship exists when one object is a specialized version
  of another object (inherits all properties of the base class, and extends it).
- Polymorphism is an object's ability to take different forms
  - Overridden methods in subclasses should be invoked if polymorphic


